sir bob neal thanks very much indeed for  speaking to us why do you think it's  time for boris johnson to go  well because i promise at the beginning  to my constituents is that i would weigh  up uh the details the contents of the  sugro report and listen to  any explanations which the prime  minister gave i've done that and i've  reflected on them and i'm afraid i  didn't regard the explanations as being  credible there was a course of conduct  that took place here in number 10  downstream the pattern of behavior um  over a number of months which clearly  breached the rules  and which  was offensive i think to many many  people who are doing the right thing and  trying to stick by the rules and we  can't have a situation where those who  are making the rules are not adhering to  themselves when they expect everybody  else to do so so that and that in turn  had led me uh to listen very carefully  to my constituents who have been saying  i'm afraid for some weeks many of them  traditional conservative voters  particularly when i was canvassing  during the local council elections but  they were saying i'm sorry we've lost  faith in the prime minister we have  faith in 2019 but we lost faith in him  now and we're losing trust in the  conservative party under his leadership  and because that trust has been  undermined i don't believe sadly that  the prime minister he is able to rebuild  it and therefore we need to move on and  find a new leader for the conservative  party  uh what is it specifically about his  explanations you didn't find credible  this time around because he he has  repeated the same explanation since day  one really uh uh what why now  well i think when we look at it we  started off um with uh a an explanation  that  no rules were broken well we now know  that they were  then we've found an explanation that  well we didn't believe they were being  broken i don't accept that anyone could  reasonably have thought that it was in  the rules to have a leaving party that  seems to be the suggestion i don't think  most people regard that as being  credible  i know quite a number of people who left  work who changed jobs during the  lockdown they didn't have leaving  parties they perhaps  said farewells on zoom or they postponed  the get-togethers until such times as  the restrictions were lifted so that  doesn't i think hold water and i don't  accept that there could have been this  course of conduct without the prime  minister having been aware at least of a  great deal of what was happening if not  every single thing so that's where i'm  left in terms of my assessment of the  credibility and that in turn leads me to  my assessment that the trust that sadly  has been lost it's a shame because he's  a man of very real talents in many ways  and i'm nothing against him personally  we've always gone on well but once the  trust is lost and that not only damages  the conservative party but it actually  damages politics as a whole and that's  it even more significant  uh and you mentioned there that your  constituents also have lost faith in  this prime minister you mentioned you  know he's a talented man a lot of people  uh fond of his character but clearly a  lot of your constituents telling you  enough is enough  and that's it that that's the reading  that i've taken from this now i speak  only for myself uh every mp has to come  to their own conclusions on that but  these are people who have been the  bedrock of conservative support uh for  years and when they say that  they're usually not far off the money  i'm sorry to say  what's the mood then in your party  tonight we've had a resignation earlier  today we've had two letters of no  confidence uh being sent um  including your own of course  what are other mps telling you you're a  much respected member of your party i'm  certain you've spoken to others  well i'm no doubt that a number of my  colleagues are very concerned at the  situation as i am but i haven't acted in  concert with anyone i came to this  decision on my own  and  according to my own assessment of the  situation so not be acting in concert  with anyone i'm not part of any campaign  uh either against anyone or for anyone  i've just taken my own decision i'm  sure that there are others  that feel  equally troubled but how they  choose to act thereafter what steps they  take  that i respect is that it's their right  to decide on  do you think we could expect more  letters of  no confidence  well uh i gotta say i i can't predict um  but there is clearly uh still real  unhappiness amongst many conservative  mps i think it is that loss of trust  that people are finding to say how my  colleagues react uh it is a matter for  their  own judgment and i'm not going to try  and second guess that for them i can  only speak for myself but i think  i'm a pretty mainstream member of the  conservative party  and people can then draw their own  conclusions but what would you like to  say to prime minister boris johnson  tonight now that you've handed in this  letter of no confidence what is your  message to him  that boris had done great service to  this country the biggest service he did  was getting us a majority in 2019 and  seeing off the threat of what i would  regard as a far left and very damaging  uh jeremy corbyn that government and  he's entitled to credit for the work  that he's done in the vaccine's rollout  and is entitled credit  for  the leadership he's shown in pulling the  western democracies together to confront  western  russian aggression in ukraine so he's  done service but sadly  now is the time to hand over the baton  because at the same time he has done  that service unfortunately other actions  have undermined that trust amongst the  electorate and trust in the system and  so it's time now to hand on and let  somebody else take over to rebuild that  trust sir bob neal mp for bromley and  chiselhurst thanks very much indeed  thank you
 

